Deinit ordering with refcounted fields
======================================

When a struct with a deinit *also* holds refcounted fields
(strings, `&T` references), the order at refcount=0 matters:

  1. **deinit body runs first.** While it runs, the struct's
     fields are still readable — including any refcounted
     buffers. The deinit can read them, copy them, log them,
     etc.

  2. **Refcounted fields are decremented after deinit returns.**
     If a string-field's refcount drops to zero, its buffer is
     freed at that point — but only *after* the deinit has had
     its chance to look at the data.

Reversing the order (decrement fields, then call deinit) would
mean the deinit reads dangling pointers. Several test scenarios:

  - A `Doc` struct with a `name: string` field; the deinit
    records the name into a module-level log. After scope exit,
    the log should contain the name.

  - A `Pair` struct holding two `&Inner` references with deinit
    counters of their own; the outer's deinit should be able to
    read the inner field values *before* the inner's deinit
    fires.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

Module-level log used by deinits to record what they saw at
deinit-time. Reset at the start of each test.

    var dwf_log: string = ""

Doc has a string field. Its deinit appends the name to the log.

    struct Doc
        name: string

    Doc.deinit() -> unit
        dwf_log = dwf_log + self.name

    drop_doc() -> unit
        val d: &Doc = new Doc("alice")
        // d goes out of scope here. deinit fires while name is still
        // a readable buffer; only afterward is the buffer's refcount
        // decremented.

    #test
    test_deinit_reads_string_field() -> unit
        dwf_log = ""
        drop_doc()
        assert(dwf_log == "alice", s"dwf_log should be 'alice', got '${dwf_log}'")

Multiple drops in sequence, each contributing to the log:

    drop_three_docs() -> unit
        val d1: &Doc = new Doc("a")
        val d2: &Doc = new Doc("b")
        val d3: &Doc = new Doc("c")

    #test
    test_multiple_deinits_each_read_their_field() -> unit
        dwf_log = ""
        drop_three_docs()
        // Order isn't pinned by the language (scope-exit drop order is
        // implementation-defined), but every name must appear exactly once.
        assert(len(dwf_log) == 3, s"dwf_log should have length 3, got ${len(dwf_log)}")
        // Check each character appears
        var has_a = false
        var has_b = false
        var has_c = false
        var i = 0
        while i < len(dwf_log)
            if dwf_log[i] == byte('a') then has_a = true
            if dwf_log[i] == byte('b') then has_b = true
            if dwf_log[i] == byte('c') then has_c = true
            i = i + 1
        assert(has_a && has_b && has_c, s"each of a/b/c should appear in '$dwf_log'")

Nested deinits — outer's deinit reads through a `&Inner` field
--------------------------------------------------------------

The outer's deinit fires while inner is still alive. The deinit
body can read fields through the still-valid struct, including
chasing through a `&Inner` reference field.

    struct DwfInner
        v: int

    struct DwfOuter
        child: &DwfInner

    DwfOuter.deinit() -> unit
        dwf_log = dwf_log + str(self.child.v)

    drop_outer() -> unit
        val o: &DwfOuter = new DwfOuter(new DwfInner(42))

    #test
    test_outer_deinit_reads_inner_through_ref_field() -> unit
        dwf_log = ""
        drop_outer()
        assert(dwf_log == "42", s"dwf_log should be '42', got '${dwf_log}'")

    // TODO: also pin "outer's drop transitively decrements its &Inner field
    // (firing inner's deinit)" once recursive ref-field-decr-on-struct-drop
    // is implemented across all seven backends. Today no backend does it
    // (the inner ref leaks on outer drop), so the test would fail uniformly
    // — it's a separate language-implementation TODO, not a per-backend bug.
